## Sprint 2 test results and coverage

Ran the full suite on `main` this evening:

    cd project
    python -m pytest --cov=core --cov-branch --cov-report=term-missing

50 tests, all passing, 13 seconds. Coverage came back at **96%**.

That numbers are not accurate though. It includes the test files themselves, and tests are always near
100%. They're 429 of the 707
statements being measured — about 61% of the total. Take them and the
migrations out and the figure for code a user can break is **94%**.

| | statements | missed | coverage |
|---|---|---|---|
| What pytest reports | 707 | 16 | 96% |
| Application code only | 222 | 12 | 94% |
| Test files | 429 | 2 | 99% |
| Migrations | 56 | 2 | 97% |

Of the twelve lines our app code misses, three are `__str__` methods on
`ShelfLife`, `Pantry` and `PantryItem` — display helpers for the admin, not
worth a test. The other nine are in one place. `views.py` is our weakest file at 88%, and the gaps are the branches
that sort items into the Freezer and the Bag (our container tests only use
Shelf and Fridge), the error handling inside the drag-and-drop view, and the
old `test-pantry/` development route that's still live.

### What this doesn't tell us

Three things worth being honest about, and we've got real examples of each
rather than textbook ones.

Coverage doesn't look at templates at all. Earlier this sprint the edit
form's `{% url %}` tag was missing its closing `%}`, so clicking Save
did nothing. Every line of `views.py` involved was covered, and
every edit test passed — because the tests POST straight to the view
instead of rendering the page and checking the form points anywhere real.

A covered line is a line that ran, not a line that's right.
`PantryItem.save()` sits at 97%, but it only works out an expiration date
when the field is empty, so dragging an item from the Fridge to the Freezer
leaves the old date sitting there. The code runs, the test passes, the
behaviour is wrong. That's filed separately.

And the gaps aren't spread evenly. The uncovered branches in `views.py` are
the drag-and-drop error handling and the Freezer/Bag routing — which is
exactly where this sprint's open bugs turned out to be. Low coverage pointed
at the right neighbourhood. It didn't find the bugs for us, and the high
coverage everywhere else didn't stop them happening.
